1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,525
A crowd of penguins stands at the edge of

2
00:00:02,525 --> 00:00:05,051
the ice. They're hungry, and the fish are right

3
00:00:05,051 --> 00:00:07,576
there, below them. But not one of them moves.

4
00:00:08,576 --> 00:00:11,708
They shuffle. They lean forward. They step back again.

5
00:00:11,708 --> 00:00:14,840
They're waiting for someone else to go first, because

6
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:17,972
somewhere in that dark water, something may be waiting

7
00:00:17,972 --> 00:00:18,320
too.

8
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:22,611
It's long, it's silver, and it's patient. Meet the

9
00:00:22,611 --> 00:00:25,903
leopard seal, the top hunter of the Antarctic coast,

10
00:00:25,903 --> 00:00:29,194
and one of the strangest predators on the planet.

11
00:00:30,194 --> 00:00:33,181
In this video, we'll find out how it's built,

12
00:00:33,181 --> 00:00:36,167
what it eats, why penguins fear it, how it

13
00:00:36,167 --> 00:00:39,154
sings, and what really happens when it meets people.

14
00:00:40,154 --> 00:00:43,445
The leopard seal's scientific name is Hydrurga leptonyx. It's

15
00:00:43,445 --> 00:00:46,737
a true seal, part of the same family as

16
00:00:46,737 --> 00:00:50,028
harbor seals and elephant seals. That means it has

17
00:00:50,028 --> 00:00:53,320
no ear flaps, just small openings on the side

18
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:54,417
of its head.

19
00:00:55,417 --> 00:00:58,699
It lives mostly in the pack ice that surrounds

20
00:00:58,699 --> 00:01:01,981
Antarctica, a vast and shifting world of floating ice

21
00:01:01,981 --> 00:01:04,899
that grows every winter and shrinks every summer.

22
00:01:05,899 --> 00:01:09,174
And the name? It comes from its coat. A

23
00:01:09,174 --> 00:01:12,448
leopard seal is silver-gray on top, paler underneath, and

24
00:01:12,448 --> 00:01:15,723
covered in dark spots, a little like a big

25
00:01:15,723 --> 00:01:16,087
cat.

26
00:01:17,087 --> 00:01:20,508
Leopard seals are big. The largest reach around 3.5

27
00:01:20,508 --> 00:01:23,929
meters, about 11 feet, and can weigh close to

28
00:01:23,929 --> 00:01:27,351
half a ton. That's roughly as heavy as seven

29
00:01:27,351 --> 00:01:28,111
grown men.

30
00:01:29,111 --> 00:01:32,601
And here's the twist. In many animals, males are

31
00:01:32,601 --> 00:01:36,092
the bigger sex. With leopard seals, it's the other

32
00:01:36,092 --> 00:01:39,194
way around. The biggest leopard seals are females.

33
00:01:40,194 --> 00:01:43,214
Then there's the head. It's enormous for a seal,

34
00:01:43,214 --> 00:01:46,233
with a long snout and a wide mouth that

35
00:01:46,233 --> 00:01:49,252
stretches back past the eyes. Some people say it

36
00:01:49,252 --> 00:01:50,930
looks almost like a reptile.

37
00:01:51,930 --> 00:01:55,280
Most true seals swim by sweeping their back flippers

38
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:58,631
side to side. Leopard seals often power through the

39
00:01:58,631 --> 00:02:01,981
water with their long front flippers instead, almost like

40
00:02:01,981 --> 00:02:02,353
wings.

41
00:02:03,353 --> 00:02:06,816
Their bodies are slender and flexible, which makes them

42
00:02:06,816 --> 00:02:10,278
fast and agile. They can twist, turn and accelerate

43
00:02:10,278 --> 00:02:13,741
in a burst, exactly what you need when your

44
00:02:13,741 --> 00:02:16,049
prey is a quick, slippery penguin.

45
00:02:17,049 --> 00:02:19,682
Most of their dives are fairly shallow, but they

46
00:02:19,682 --> 00:02:22,315
can hold their breath for many minutes when they

47
00:02:22,315 --> 00:02:22,900
need to.

48
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:27,149
Now let's look inside that mouth, because it hides

49
00:02:27,149 --> 00:02:30,399
one of nature's neatest tricks. At the front are

50
00:02:30,399 --> 00:02:33,648
long, sharp canine teeth, perfect for grabbing and holding

51
00:02:33,648 --> 00:02:34,009
prey.

52
00:02:35,009 --> 00:02:38,155
But further back are the cheek teeth. Each one

53
00:02:38,155 --> 00:02:41,302
has three sharp points. When the jaws close, those

54
00:02:41,302 --> 00:02:44,448
points lock together and leave tiny gaps between them,

55
00:02:44,448 --> 00:02:46,895
like the holes in a kitchen strainer.

56
00:02:47,895 --> 00:02:50,827
That lets a leopard seal take a big gulp

57
00:02:50,827 --> 00:02:53,760
of water full of krill, the tiny shrimp-like animals

58
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:56,692
that swarm in the Southern Ocean, and then push

59
00:02:56,692 --> 00:02:59,624
the water back out while the krill stay inside.

60
00:03:00,624 --> 00:03:03,831
Think about that. The same animal that hunts penguins

61
00:03:03,831 --> 00:03:07,038
can also feed a little like a whale. One

62
00:03:07,038 --> 00:03:09,532
mouth, two completely different ways to eat.

63
00:03:10,532 --> 00:03:13,236
So what does a leopard seal actually eat? Pretty

64
00:03:13,236 --> 00:03:15,940
much whatever the season offers. Krill can make up

65
00:03:15,940 --> 00:03:18,643
a big part of the diet at certain times

66
00:03:18,643 --> 00:03:21,347
of the year. Fish and squid are on the

67
00:03:21,347 --> 00:03:21,948
menu too.

68
00:03:22,948 --> 00:03:26,029
Then there are the penguins. A penguin is fast

69
00:03:26,029 --> 00:03:29,110
underwater, but a leopard seal only has to win

70
00:03:29,110 --> 00:03:29,452
once.

71
00:03:30,452 --> 00:03:33,773
And leopard seals also hunt other seals, especially young

72
00:03:33,773 --> 00:03:37,093
ones. Many crabeater seals carry long scars on their

73
00:03:37,093 --> 00:03:40,414
bodies, thought to come from leopard seal attacks they

74
00:03:40,414 --> 00:03:42,259
survived when they were young.

75
00:03:43,259 --> 00:03:46,245
Leopard seals can't chew big prey into small bites.

76
00:03:46,245 --> 00:03:49,231
So with a large catch, they often shake it

77
00:03:49,231 --> 00:03:52,217
hard at the surface to tear it into pieces

78
00:03:52,217 --> 00:03:53,212
they can swallow.

79
00:03:54,212 --> 00:03:57,089
The leopard seal's favorite weapon is patience. It often

80
00:03:57,089 --> 00:03:59,967
lingers close to the ice edge, right where penguins

81
00:03:59,967 --> 00:04:02,205
have to enter and leave the water.

82
00:04:03,205 --> 00:04:06,177
It can wait quietly near the surface, hard to

83
00:04:06,177 --> 00:04:09,149
spot from above. To a penguin standing on the

84
00:04:09,149 --> 00:04:11,460
ice, the water may look completely empty.

85
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:15,736
That's why penguins hesitate. And when they finally jump,

86
00:04:15,736 --> 00:04:19,011
they often go together. A crowd of birds splashing

87
00:04:19,011 --> 00:04:22,287
in at once is much harder to target than

88
00:04:22,287 --> 00:04:23,379
one penguin alone.

89
00:04:24,379 --> 00:04:27,463
The same danger waits on the way home. Coming

90
00:04:27,463 --> 00:04:30,546
back out of the water, penguins often launch themselves

91
00:04:30,546 --> 00:04:33,287
onto the ice as fast as they can.

92
00:04:34,287 --> 00:04:38,310
Here's something most people never hear. Leopard seals sing.

93
00:04:39,310 --> 00:04:43,350
During the breeding season, males produce long, low, eerie

94
00:04:43,350 --> 00:04:47,391
calls underwater, repeating them again and again. To human

95
00:04:47,391 --> 00:04:51,431
ears, they sound almost like something from science fiction.

96
00:04:52,431 --> 00:04:55,343
Scientists record these songs with underwater microphones. Because leopard

97
00:04:55,343 --> 00:04:58,255
seals are so hard to find and count, listening

98
00:04:58,255 --> 00:05:01,168
to them is one of the best ways to

99
00:05:01,168 --> 00:05:02,462
learn where they are.

100
00:05:03,462 --> 00:05:06,698
Leopard seals are mostly loners. You'll usually see them

101
00:05:06,698 --> 00:05:09,575
alone, resting on an ice floe between hunts.

102
00:05:10,575 --> 00:05:13,231
Females give birth on the pack ice, usually in

103
00:05:13,231 --> 00:05:15,886
the southern summer. The pup nurses for a few

104
00:05:15,886 --> 00:05:18,542
weeks, then has to learn to hunt for itself.

105
00:05:19,542 --> 00:05:23,178
As adults, leopard seals have very few natural enemies.

106
00:05:23,178 --> 00:05:25,602
Killer whales are the main one.

107
00:05:26,602 --> 00:05:29,717
In the wild, leopard seals can live for more

108
00:05:29,717 --> 00:05:30,755
than two decades.

109
00:05:31,755 --> 00:05:35,148
People have been meeting leopard seals for a long

110
00:05:35,148 --> 00:05:38,542
time. In 1916, during Ernest Shackleton's famous expedition, the

111
00:05:38,542 --> 00:05:41,935
crew were camped on drifting sea ice after their

112
00:05:41,935 --> 00:05:43,066
ship was crushed.

113
00:05:44,066 --> 00:05:47,480
One crew member, Thomas Orde-Lees, was chased across the

114
00:05:47,480 --> 00:05:50,893
ice by a leopard seal. Another expedition member shot

115
00:05:50,893 --> 00:05:53,548
the animal before it could reach him.

116
00:05:54,548 --> 00:05:58,618
Today, divers and scientists meet leopard seals far more

117
00:05:58,618 --> 00:06:02,687
often. And most of those encounters are about curiosity.

118
00:06:02,687 --> 00:06:06,757
The seal circles, watches, opens its mouth, and eventually

119
00:06:06,757 --> 00:06:07,661
swims away.

120
00:06:08,661 --> 00:06:11,953
Some are downright strange. One wildlife photographer described a

121
00:06:11,953 --> 00:06:15,244
female that kept bringing him penguins, again and again,

122
00:06:15,244 --> 00:06:18,170
as if she was trying to feed him.

123
00:06:19,170 --> 00:06:22,821
But leopard seals are powerful wild predators, and that

124
00:06:22,821 --> 00:06:26,473
should never be forgotten. In 2003, a leopard seal

125
00:06:26,473 --> 00:06:30,124
killed British marine biologist Kirsty Brown while she was

126
00:06:30,124 --> 00:06:32,153
snorkeling near her research station.

127
00:06:33,153 --> 00:06:36,327
It's the only known human death caused by the

128
00:06:36,327 --> 00:06:39,501
species. It led to stricter safety rules for scientists

129
00:06:39,501 --> 00:06:42,675
working in Antarctic waters, including closer watch for seals

130
00:06:42,675 --> 00:06:44,438
before anyone enters the water.

131
00:06:45,438 --> 00:06:49,138
Leopard seals don't always stay in the far south.

132
00:06:49,138 --> 00:06:52,838
Young seals in particular sometimes wander thousands of kilometers

133
00:06:52,838 --> 00:06:53,249
north.

134
00:06:54,249 --> 00:06:57,633
They've turned up on the coasts of New Zealand,

135
00:06:57,633 --> 00:07:01,017
Australia, South America and South Africa. In New Zealand,

136
00:07:01,017 --> 00:07:04,401
they're now seen often enough that some are treated

137
00:07:04,401 --> 00:07:06,657
as residents rather than rare visitors.

138
00:07:07,657 --> 00:07:10,537
When a leopard seal hauls out on a busy

139
00:07:10,537 --> 00:07:13,417
beach, the advice is simple: give it plenty of

140
00:07:13,417 --> 00:07:15,337
space, and keep dogs well away.

141
00:07:16,337 --> 00:07:20,122
Studying an animal that lives scattered across a frozen

142
00:07:20,122 --> 00:07:23,907
ocean is hard work. Researchers often start by simply

143
00:07:23,907 --> 00:07:27,692
searching for seals resting on the ice, from boats,

144
00:07:27,692 --> 00:07:28,954
ships and aircraft.

145
00:07:29,954 --> 00:07:32,821
Some leopard seals have been fitted with small tags

146
00:07:32,821 --> 00:07:35,687
that track where they travel and how deep they

147
00:07:35,687 --> 00:07:38,554
dive. When the seal molts, the tag falls off

148
00:07:38,554 --> 00:07:39,828
with the old fur.

149
00:07:40,828 --> 00:07:44,565
Others have carried tiny cameras, giving scientists a seal's-eye

150
00:07:44,565 --> 00:07:48,302
view of how it searches, chases and catches its

151
00:07:48,302 --> 00:07:48,717
food.

152
00:07:49,717 --> 00:07:53,023
And some of the best clues are hiding in

153
00:07:53,023 --> 00:07:56,330
what the seals leave behind. By studying their droppings,

154
00:07:56,330 --> 00:07:59,636
scientists can identify bones, beaks and even DNA from

155
00:07:59,636 --> 00:08:01,106
the animals they ate.

156
00:08:02,106 --> 00:08:05,090
Leopard seals have a scary reputation, so let's clear

157
00:08:05,090 --> 00:08:06,416
up a few myths.

158
00:08:07,416 --> 00:08:11,023
Myth number one: leopard seals mostly eat penguins. In

159
00:08:11,023 --> 00:08:14,631
reality, their diet changes with the season and the

160
00:08:14,631 --> 00:08:18,238
place. Krill, fish and other seals can matter just

161
00:08:18,238 --> 00:08:19,040
as much.

162
00:08:20,040 --> 00:08:23,637
Myth number two: every leopard seal that approaches a

163
00:08:23,637 --> 00:08:27,234
diver is attacking. Most are curious. But because a

164
00:08:27,234 --> 00:08:30,831
real attack can come without warning, divers still treat

165
00:08:30,831 --> 00:08:32,030
every encounter seriously.

166
00:08:33,030 --> 00:08:36,120
And myth number three: leopard seals and polar bears

167
00:08:36,120 --> 00:08:39,210
might meet. They never do. Polar bears live in

168
00:08:39,210 --> 00:08:42,299
the Arctic, at the top of the planet. Leopard

169
00:08:42,299 --> 00:08:45,046
seals live in the Antarctic, at the bottom.

170
00:08:46,046 --> 00:08:49,213
Leopard seals depend on sea ice. They rest on

171
00:08:49,213 --> 00:08:52,381
it, give birth on it, and molt on it.

172
00:08:52,381 --> 00:08:55,548
And their food web depends on it too, especially

173
00:08:55,548 --> 00:08:58,715
the krill that so many Antarctic animals rely on.

174
00:08:59,715 --> 00:09:02,607
In recent years, Antarctic sea ice has reached some

175
00:09:02,607 --> 00:09:05,498
of its lowest levels on record. Scientists are watching

176
00:09:05,498 --> 00:09:08,390
closely to understand what that means for animals like

177
00:09:08,390 --> 00:09:09,354
the leopard seal.

178
00:09:10,354 --> 00:09:13,454
The surprising part? Nobody knows exactly how many leopard

179
00:09:13,454 --> 00:09:16,555
seals there are. They live scattered across a huge,

180
00:09:16,555 --> 00:09:19,656
icy ocean, which makes them one of the hardest

181
00:09:19,656 --> 00:09:21,378
seals on Earth to count.

182
00:09:22,378 --> 00:09:25,415
So the next time you see penguins hesitating at

183
00:09:25,415 --> 00:09:28,452
the edge of the ice, you'll know why. They're

184
00:09:28,452 --> 00:09:30,476
not being shy. They're being smart.

185
00:09:31,476 --> 00:09:34,767
The leopard seal isn't a monster. It's a brilliant,

186
00:09:34,767 --> 00:09:38,059
flexible hunter, built for one of the harshest places

187
00:09:38,059 --> 00:09:38,790
on Earth.

188
00:09:39,790 --> 00:09:42,562
Which animal should we explore next? Tell us in

189
00:09:42,562 --> 00:09:45,333
the comments, and we'll see you in the next

190
00:09:45,333 --> 00:09:45,641
one.
